<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=dugout-canoe">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dugout canoe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8040–7510 BCE</span> · Also known as logboat, monoxylon, Einbaum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A dugout canoe, or logboat, is a boat made from a hollowed-out tree, and the oldest boat type archaeologists have found. Before metal tools the log was hollowed with controlled fires and the burnt wood removed with an adze. The Pesse canoe from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, carbon dated to between 8040 and 7510 BCE, is believed to be the world's oldest boat; the Dufuna canoe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and a dugout from Kuahuqiao in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, are both about 8,000 years old. Dugouts could be fitted with outriggers for rougher water, and they are still paddled and punted on lakes and rivers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dugout-canoe.jpg" alt="Dugout canoe" width="330" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rumuli · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Traditional_Boat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>8040–7510 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Pesse, Netherlands</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>Dufuna, Nigeria (c. 8,000 years ago); Kuahuqiao, Zhejiang, China (c. 8,000 years ago)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Dugouts may be the oldest boats found only because their massive wood survives better than lighter craft such as bark canoes.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">Stone tools<span>3.3 million years ago</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/control-of-fire">Control of fire<span>c. 1 million years ago</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=dugout-canoe">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
